## PART II: DIASPORA AND SURVIVAL

### Chapter 4 — Judaism Without a Temple: The Rabbinic Revolution

Out of the vacuum left by the Temple's destruction emerged a bold and adaptive response in the form of rabbinic Judaism. The shift from Second Temple Judaism to rabbinic Judaism was not a mere chronological transition but, as scholar Shaye Cohen describes, a substantive change marking the end of one world and the beginning of another. [Bart D. Ehrman](https://www.bartehrman.com/second-temple-judaism/)

Rabbinic literature attributes much of the initial effort to reimagine Jewish life to Rabbi Yohanan ben Zakkai, who negotiated with Roman authorities to establish a center for learning and religious leadership at Yavne, a small town on the southern coast of the Land of Israel. [Bart D. Ehrman](https://www.bartehrman.com/second-temple-judaism/) This founding gesture — the substitution of a *yeshiva* for a Temple, of scholarly disputation for sacrificial worship — was an act of civilization-building of the highest order. In transforming the catastrophe of 70 CE into the occasion for the creation of a new form of Judaism centered on text, interpretation, and communal law, the Rabbis created a portable civilization capable of surviving and flourishing anywhere in the world.

The great texts of Rabbinic Judaism — the Mishnah, compiled by Rabbi Judah ha-Nasi around 200 CE, and the two Talmuds, the Palestinian and the Babylonian — are not merely legal codes. They are records of centuries of intellectual conversation, argument, storytelling, ethical reflection, and communal self-definition. Rabbinic Judaism, centered around communal synagogue worship and Torah study, eventually evolved out of the Pharisaic school and became the mainstream form of Jewish religion, codified definitively with the completion of the Babylonian Talmud in the sixth century CE. [epfl](https://graphsearch.epfl.ch/en/concept/323316/concepts?page=1) The Talmud's characteristic mode — the preservation of minority opinions alongside majority rulings, the elevation of argument itself as a religious value, the insistence that the legitimate interpretation of God's word is conducted through human reason in dialogue — created an intellectual culture whose effects can be traced in the history of Jewish scholarship, philosophy, and eventually science across the subsequent two millennia.

### Chapter 5 — Jewish Communities Across Antiquity

**Babylonia, Alexandria, and the Mediterranean World**

The Jewish world of late antiquity was a genuinely international phenomenon. From the academies of Babylonia, which became the pre-eminent centers of Jewish scholarship after the consolidation of Roman power over the Land of Israel, to the vast community of Alexandria, to the scattered but interconnected communities of Asia Minor, Greece, Italy, and North Africa, Jews participated in the commercial, intellectual, and cultural life of the ancient world while maintaining the distinctive institutions — synagogue, school, communal court, calendar — that preserved their separate identity.

The Jewish community in Babylon continued to flourish throughout the Second Temple period and beyond, and after the Roman Empire shut down the Sanhedrin in 363 CE, this was where the center of Jewish rabbinic authority came to rest. [Aish](https://aish.com/48949881/) The great Babylonian academies of Sura and Pumbedita, active for nearly a millennium, produced the Babylonian Talmud and generations of *geonim* — the authoritative rabbinic scholars whose *responsa* (legal opinions in response to questions from Jewish communities around the world) constituted the first global system of Jewish legal coordination.

The community of Alexandria represents a different model: deeply Hellenized, philosophically sophisticated, socially integrated into the broader civic life of one of the ancient world's great cosmopolitan cities. The Alexandrian community produced the Septuagint and the philosophical writings of Philo, but it also suffered devastating violence in the anti-Jewish riots of 38 CE and the catastrophic Jewish revolt of 115–117 CE, which effectively ended the great Alexandrian Jewish community. The history of Alexandria's Jews is thus a history of both extraordinary achievement and devastating vulnerability — a combination that would recur in Jewish communities across the world.

### Chapter 6 — Jews Under Christianity and Islam

The transformation of Christianity from a Jewish sect into the official religion of the Roman Empire in the fourth century CE had profound and lasting consequences for Jewish communities throughout the Mediterranean world. Jewish-Christian relations, which had been complex and often tense since the parting of the ways in the first and second centuries CE, were now structured by an asymmetry of power: Jews became a legally subordinate minority within a Christian polity that at once preserved them (as witnesses to the truth of scripture) and restricted, humiliated, and periodically persecuted them. The canonical restrictions of Roman and later medieval Christian law — Jews were barred from holding public office, from owning Christian slaves, from constructing new synagogues, from intermarrying with Christians — defined the parameters of Jewish life in Christian Europe for more than a millennium.

The rise of Islam in the seventh century CE created a very different framework for Jewish life. The Islamic concept of the *dhimmi* — the protected non-Muslim living under Muslim rule — afforded Jews a formally subordinate but legally recognized and often practically viable status within the emerging Islamic world. In the great cities of the Islamic caliphates — Baghdad, Cairo, Córdoba, Fez — Jewish communities often flourished as merchants, physicians, philosophers, and financiers. The Arabic language became the primary vehicle of Jewish intellectual life for several centuries; Jewish philosophers wrote in Arabic, absorbed the traditions of Greek philosophy as transmitted through Arabic scholarship, and participated in the extraordinary intellectual culture of the Islamic golden age. The contrast between the relative openness of the Islamic world and the more systematically oppressive conditions of much of medieval Christian Europe was real, though it should not be idealized: Jews in Islamic lands also faced periods of persecution, forced conversion, and destruction.

