## PART IV: PERSECUTION AND RESILIENCE

### Chapter 10 — Medieval Anti-Judaism

The history of the Jewish experience in medieval Christian Europe is a history that must be confronted with full honesty, however painful that confrontation may be. The theological anti-Judaism that was embedded in Christian teaching from the early centuries of the common era — the charge of deicide, the identification of Jews as enemies of Christ and of Christian society — provided the ideological foundation for centuries of legal discrimination, social exclusion, and organized violence.

The Crusades, launched in 1096, brought the first large-scale massacres of Jewish communities in the Rhineland and beyond. Crusaders who were journeying to "liberate" Jerusalem asked themselves why they should travel to distant lands to fight God's enemies when they could destroy them in their own communities. The massacres of Speyer, Worms, and Mainz, carried out against communities that had lived in those cities for generations, mark one of the darkest chapters in medieval European history. The Jewish communities of the Rhineland responded to these massacres with *kiddush Hashem* — the sanctification of God's name through martyrdom — and their chronicles of suffering became foundational texts in the Jewish memory of persecution.

The twelfth and thirteenth centuries saw the proliferation of the blood libel — the accusation that Jews murdered Christian children to use their blood in the Passover ritual — a charge so patently absurd that multiple popes explicitly condemned it, yet one that proved extraordinarily durable and lethal. Accusations of ritual murder led to the execution of entire communities. The accusation that Jews desecrated the Eucharistic host circulated widely from the thirteenth century onward. The expulsion of Jews from England in 1290, from France in 1306 (and again in 1394), and from various German cities across the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries progressively narrowed the geography of Jewish life in Western Europe.

### Chapter 11 — The Expulsion from Spain

On March 31, 1492, the joint Catholic monarchs of Spain, Ferdinand II of Aragon and Isabella I of Castile, issued an edict of expulsion for the Jews of Spain. All Jews refusing to convert were to leave by the end of July and never return, under penalty of death. [The National Library of Israel](https://www.nli.org.il/en/discover/judaism/jewish-history/spain-jews-expulsion)

The Alhambra Decree was both a religious and an economic catastrophe of the first order. Within the year, some 175,000 Jews left Spain, while those who remained were forced to renounce their faith. [Museum of Jewish Heritage](https://mjhnyc.org/blog/1492-letter-regarding-jewish-property-in-spain/) The Ottoman Sultan Bayezid II's reported response to Ferdinand's action — that by expelling such capable subjects, Ferdinand had impoverished his own kingdom to enrich others — was not merely a witticism but a precise economic analysis. Sultan Bayezid II sent the Ottoman navy under the command of Kemal Reis to aid the expelled Jews, issued proclamations throughout his empire that refugees were to be welcomed, granted them permission to settle and become Ottoman citizens, and ridiculed the conduct of the Spanish monarchs. [History of Information](https://www.historyofinformation.com/detail.php?id=2346)

Scholars disagree about how many Jews left Spain after the expulsion; estimates range from 50,000 to 300,000. Many initially crossed the border into Portugal, from which they were subsequently expelled in 1497. Some went to Palestine. Others traveled to England, the Netherlands, and the Mediterranean region. Sephardic Jews established communities throughout North Africa, where they remained into the late twentieth century. [Trio Sefardi](https://triosefardi.com/sephardic-history/the-sephardic-diaspora/)

Tens of thousands of Sephardic Jews settled in Ottoman territories, mainly Istanbul and Thessaloniki, upon the invitation of Sultan Bayezid II, and the city of Thessaloniki came to be known as "the Jerusalem of the Balkans." [Anadolu Ajansı](https://www.aa.com.tr/en/europe/ottoman-lands-provided-safe-haven-for-sephardic-jews-expelled-from-spain/2651488)

The expulsion from Spain represents not merely a demographic displacement but a cultural catastrophe and a cultural gift. The Sephardic communities of North Africa, the Ottoman Empire, Italy, and the Netherlands carried with them the Hebrew poetry, liturgical music, religious scholarship, and — above all — the Ladino language that preserved within it the sounds of a medieval Spain they had been forced to leave. Ladino, the Judeo-Spanish language that developed and spread throughout the Mediterranean basin following the expulsion, preserved not only the Castilian Spanish of fifteenth-century Spain but also the liturgical hymns, musical traditions, and cultural practices of Iberian Jewish life. [The National Library of Israel](https://www.nli.org.il/en/discover/judaism/jewish-history/spain-jews-expulsion)

### Chapter 12 — Pogroms and Survival

The Jews of Eastern Europe — concentrated in the territories of Poland, Lithuania, Ukraine, and the Russian Empire — constituted by the eighteenth century the largest concentration of Jews in the world. The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth had, from the sixteenth century onward, offered Jewish communities a degree of legal autonomy and institutional self-governance — centered on the institution of the *kehilla* (communal council) and the *Va'ad Arba Aratzot* (Council of the Four Lands) — that was unusual in the European context. This autonomy fostered the development of a rich and distinctive culture: the Yiddish language and literature, the Hasidic movement and its spiritual revolution, the great academies (*yeshivot*) of Vilna, Volozhin, and Poznan, and the complex urban culture of Jewish merchants, artisans, and scholars.

But this world was also one of profound vulnerability. The massacres carried out by the Cossack forces of Bogdan Khmelnytsky in the Ukraine between 1648 and 1649 killed tens of thousands of Jews and destroyed hundreds of communities — an event so catastrophic that it was commemorated in rabbinic literature as second only to the destruction of the Temple. The progressive partitions of Poland in the late eighteenth century incorporated the majority of Ashkenazi Jewry into the Russian Empire, which responded to this demographic challenge with the institution of the Pale of Settlement — a defined territory within which Jews were legally required to reside — and with periodic pogroms that drove hundreds of thousands of Jews westward and across the Atlantic in the great migration of the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries.

