## PART V: MOROCCAN JEWRY — A UNIQUE HISTORY

### Chapter 13 — The Ancient Jewish Presence in Morocco

The history of the Jews in Morocco goes back to ancient times, with the oldest irrefutable evidence of Judaism in Morocco dating to the Roman period. After the Muslim conquest of the Maghreb, Jews became the primary religious minority group. [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Jews_in_Morocco)

The Jewish population of Morocco actually preceded the Arab emigration to the country, so we can say that Jews are among its oldest settlers. They lived together with the Berber population, the older residents of North Africa, and in the early period were fully integrated. [Jhtravel](https://jhtravel.org/moroccos-jewish-history-highlights-from-a-scholar/) It is possible that the first Jews arrived as early as the sixth century BCE, perhaps following the destruction of the First Temple in Jerusalem. More arrived with Phoenician traders and later during Roman rule. The Roman city of Volubilis, on the Atlantic coast north of Rabat, had a documented Jewish community. [Gil Travel](https://www.giltravel.com/blog/history-of-moroccan-jews-chronicles-of-faith-survival-legacy/)

The question of the earliest Jewish settlement in Morocco is one that intersects with the broader question of Berber-Jewish relations, which represents one of the most fascinating and under-studied chapters of North African history. Ancient traditions, some of considerable scholarly credibility and others more legendary in character, speak of Berber tribes who adopted Judaism in antiquity — traditions that resonate with the famous story of the Kahina, the Berber warrior-queen who led resistance to the Arab conquest of North Africa in the late seventh century CE and whom some sources describe as Jewish or as heading a largely Jewish force. Whatever the precise historical reality, these traditions attest to a deep and ancient entanglement between Jewish and Berber identities in the Maghreb that long predates the Arab conquest.

### Chapter 14 — Jews and Muslims in Moroccan Society

For many centuries, Moroccan Jews lived in the mellah — the Jewish quarter whose synagogues were always adjacent to the royal palace, usually in the center of the old city — and were protected by the royal authority. [Jhtravel](https://jhtravel.org/moroccos-jewish-history-highlights-from-a-scholar/)

The first Mellah of Morocco was created in Fez. Fez had long hosted the largest and one of the oldest Jewish communities in Morocco, present since the city's foundation by the Idrisids in the late eighth or early ninth century. A Jewish community was concentrated in the neighborhood known as "Caravanserai of the Jew" near Bab Guissa in the northeast of the city since the time of Idris II in the early ninth century. [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mellah)

The mellah was an institution of genuine ambiguity. The establishment of the Jewish mellah in Morocco had dual purposes: the protection of the Jewish population and the enforcement of a degree of segregation. [Days Morocco Tours](https://www.daysmoroccotours.com/the-jewish-mellah-in-morocco/) Yet within its walls, the mellah was a world of remarkable cultural vitality. Life in the Jewish mellah was characterized by a strong sense of community and resilience. The narrow streets were lined with homes, synagogues, schools, and bustling markets. Synagogues were at the heart of these neighborhoods, serving as places of worship, learning, and community gatherings. [Days Morocco Tours](https://www.daysmoroccotours.com/the-jewish-mellah-in-morocco/)

The relations between Jewish and Muslim Moroccan communities were governed by a complex code of proximity and difference. Jews and Muslims shared urban spaces, markets, languages, and many cultural practices while maintaining distinct religious identities. Jews served as goldsmiths, silversmiths, jewelers, textile merchants, and moneylenders — occupations that complemented rather than competed with the economic roles of their Muslim neighbors. Musical traditions, culinary practices, and folk beliefs crossed communal boundaries in both directions. The Judeo-Moroccan dialect, a form of Arabic incorporating Hebrew elements and distinctive phonological features, was itself a testimony to the depth of cultural interpenetration.

### Chapter 15 — Jewish Contributions to Moroccan Civilization

The Jewish communities in the mellah were renowned for their economic contributions. Skilled in various crafts, they produced exquisite jewelry, textiles, and leather goods. [Days Morocco Tours](https://www.daysmoroccotours.com/the-jewish-mellah-in-morocco/) But the contributions of Moroccan Jews to the civilization of Morocco extended far beyond craft production. Jewish merchants served as intermediaries in the trans-Saharan trade, linking Morocco to the economies of sub-Saharan Africa and to the commercial networks of the Mediterranean and Atlantic worlds. Jewish physicians served at royal courts and in urban communities. Jewish diplomats and interpreters, fluent in multiple languages and familiar with multiple legal and cultural systems, served as indispensable intermediaries between Moroccan sultans and European powers.

The contribution of Moroccan Jews to music deserves particular emphasis. The Andalusian musical tradition — *al-ala* — which was carried to Morocco by the refugees of 1492, was preserved and transmitted in large part through the work of Jewish musicians, many of whom served as professional performers at court and in the households of the Moroccan elite. The great families of Moroccan Jewish musicians — the Bensoussan, the Elkayam, the Benarroch — maintained this tradition across generations. The integration of Hebrew liturgical elements into Moroccan musical forms, and of Moroccan musical styles into Jewish liturgy, created a distinctive Judeo-Moroccan musical culture that is one of the great artistic heritages of the Mediterranean world.

### Chapter 16 — The Alaouite Period and Royal Protection

The relationship between the Alaouite dynasty — which has ruled Morocco since the seventeenth century — and the Jewish community of Morocco represents one of the most significant examples in the Islamic world of sustained royal protection of a Jewish minority. The Alaouite sultans, who traced their lineage to the Prophet Muhammad, nonetheless recognized the importance of the Jewish community to the economic and diplomatic life of Morocco and generally extended to it a degree of protection that distinguished the Moroccan Jewish experience from that of Jewish communities in many other parts of the world.

The institution of the *tujjar al-sultan* — the "merchants of the sultan" — formalized the role of Jewish merchants as commercial agents of the crown, affording them special protections and legal status in exchange for their services. Several Moroccan sultans employed Jewish diplomats and counselors to conduct sensitive negotiations with European powers, recognizing that the transnational networks and multilingual competencies of Jewish merchants made them uniquely valuable intermediaries.

The tradition of royal embrace of the Jewish community was transmitted from sultan to sultan. Mohammed VI, the current ruler, counts Andre Azulay, a leader of the Jewish community, among his principal advisors — a continuity of royal-Jewish relations extending across centuries. [Jhtravel](https://jhtravel.org/moroccos-jewish-history-highlights-from-a-scholar/)

### Chapter 17 — Colonialism and Transformation

The establishment of the French Protectorate over Morocco in 1912 transformed the conditions of Jewish life in profound and sometimes contradictory ways. When France established its protectorate, Jews embraced the modernizing influence of European rule. They adopted French language and customs while holding fast to their own traditions. Tens of thousands moved to the new city of Casablanca and went into professions like banking, retail, and teaching. [Medium](https://medium.com/@adil.faouzi2002/the-rich-history-of-moroccan-jews-77abede39a38)

The Alliance Israélite Universelle, the French-Jewish educational organization founded in 1860, established a network of schools throughout the Moroccan Jewish community that provided a French-language education to tens of thousands of Jewish children, integrating them into the structures of modernity while sometimes straining their connections to traditional communal life. The Alliance schools produced generations of bilingual, professionally trained Moroccan Jews who played central roles in the expanding urban economy of colonial Morocco.

But colonialism also created new vulnerabilities. In August 1941, the French Vichy regime promulgated laws discriminating against Moroccan Jews: new laws set quotas on the number of Jewish doctors and lawyers, expelled students from French schools, and attempted to relocate Jews into the mellah. [World Jewish Congress](https://www.worldjewishcongress.org/en/about/communities/MA) The role of Sultan Mohammed V, who is widely remembered in Moroccan Jewish memory as having refused to enforce the Vichy anti-Jewish laws and as having affirmed the status of Jews as full Moroccan subjects, remains a powerful symbol of the special relationship between the Alaouite monarchy and its Jewish community — though historians continue to debate the precise nature and extent of the Sultan's resistance.

### Chapter 18 — Independence and the Moroccan Jewish Diaspora

The achievement of Moroccan independence in 1956, and the waves of emigration that followed — to Israel, to France, to Canada, and to other destinations — transformed Moroccan Jewish life from a community of approximately 250,000 in the early 1950s to the roughly 2,500 who remain in Morocco today. "To this very day, Moroccans speak longingly about the Jewish community. One question is: Why did they leave? And why did they leave in those numbers? Meaning there's almost no one left." [Jhtravel](https://jhtravel.org/moroccos-jewish-history-highlights-from-a-scholar/)

The causes of emigration were multiple and interacting: the draw of Israel and the Zionist project, anti-Jewish violence associated with the First Arab-Israeli War, the pressures of Moroccan nationalism, economic disruption, and the simple pull of communities that had already established themselves abroad. The departure of the Moroccan Jewish community was not a single event but a process extending over several decades, shaped by different factors in different periods and profoundly affecting both those who left and the country they left behind.

The Moroccan Jewish diaspora — in Israel (where Moroccan Jews and their descendants constitute one of the largest ethnic communities), in France, in Canada, and elsewhere — has maintained a distinctive identity grounded in the culture, music, cuisine, and religious traditions of Morocco. The harkia (pilgrimage) to the shrines of Moroccan Jewish saints, practiced by diaspora communities around the world, attests to the enduring hold of Moroccan Jewish culture and the depth of attachment to the land that was home for so many centuries.

