## PART VI: MODERNITY, EMANCIPATION, AND CRISIS

### Chapter 19 — Enlightenment and Emancipation

The European Enlightenment of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries posed to the Jewish question a new framework: the possibility of civic emancipation — the extension of full citizenship rights to Jews — in exchange for the "regeneration" of the Jews, their transformation from a separate nation into citizens of a secular state who happened to practice a different religion. The debate over Jewish emancipation, conducted across Europe from the late eighteenth century onward, forced both Jewish and non-Jewish thinkers to confront fundamental questions about the nature of citizenship, the relationship between religious and civic identity, and the conditions under which a minority can be integrated into a majority society.

Moses Mendelssohn (1729–1786), the German Jewish philosopher, sought to demonstrate through his philosophical writings and his translation of the Hebrew Bible into German that Jews could be fully modern Europeans without ceasing to be Jews — that Jewish religious identity was compatible with participation in the universal culture of enlightened reason. His circle of Jewish followers, and the broader movement of Jewish enlightenment (*Haskalah*) that his work inspired, sought to reform Jewish education, promote the study of secular subjects alongside traditional texts, and integrate Jewish life into the mainstream of European cultural and economic life.

The French Revolution of 1789 extended citizenship to French Jews in 1791, initiating a process of emancipation that spread gradually across Europe over the following century. Emancipation transformed Jewish life in ways both liberating and disorienting. Jews entered European universities, professions, cultural institutions, and political life with remarkable speed and distinction. But emancipation also created new pressures: assimilation, conversion, and the erosion of traditional communal life accompanied the new opportunities. And the promise of civic equality was never fully or lastingly redeemed: anti-Semitism persisted and in some contexts intensified in the age of emancipation, now taking new ideological forms adapted to the vocabulary of modern nationalism and pseudo-scientific racism.

### Chapter 20 — Jewish Contributions to Modern Science and Culture

The disproportionate Jewish contribution to modern science, philosophy, literature, medicine, and economics is one of the most remarkable and most discussed phenomena in the history of civilization. Since the first Nobel Prizes were awarded in 1895, approximately 20 percent of recipients — over 200 individuals — have been Jewish, an extraordinary figure given that Jews constitute only about 0.2 percent of the world's population. [My Jewish Learning](https://www.myjewishlearning.com/article/eight-famous-jewish-nobel-laureates/)

From 1870 to 1950, Jewish representation in literature was four times what one would expect from population size; in music, five times; in biology, eight times; in chemistry, six times; in physics, nine times; in mathematics, twelve times; and in philosophy, fourteen times. [American Enterprise Institute](https://www.aei.org/articles/jewish-genius/)

Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity, Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory of the unconscious, Karl Marx's critique of political economy, Émile Durkheim's foundations of sociology, Franz Boas's transformation of anthropology, and Hannah Arendt's political philosophy represent, between them, some of the most consequential intellectual contributions to the modern world. In literature, Marcel Proust, Franz Kafka, Saul Bellow, Philip Roth, and Elie Wiesel created works that have shaped the sensibility of the twentieth century. In music, Gustav Mahler, Arnold Schoenberg, Leonard Bernstein, and Bob Dylan transformed their respective genres. In medicine, the development of antibiotics by Selman Waksman, the polio vaccine by Jonas Salk, and the contributions of dozens of Jewish physicians and researchers to the understanding and treatment of human disease have saved tens of millions of lives.

The explanation for this disproportionate contribution has been much debated. The culture of study and interpretation fostered by the rabbinic tradition, the premium placed on literacy and learning within Jewish communal life, the experience of living as a minority that required constant adaptation and creativity, and the particular stimulus of emancipation — the release of enormous intellectual energies that had previously been confined to traditional channels — have all been proposed as contributing factors. What is not in serious dispute is the reality and the magnitude of the contribution itself.

### Chapter 21 — Nationalism and Modern Antisemitism

The nineteenth and early twentieth centuries witnessed the paradoxical coexistence of Jewish emancipation and the rise of a new, more virulent form of antisemitism. Where medieval anti-Judaism had been primarily theological — Jews as Christ-killers, desecrators of the host, practitioners of ritual murder — modern antisemitism drew on the vocabulary of nationalism, racial science, and social Darwinism to construct a secular but no less lethal image of the Jew as racial enemy, as agent of international conspiracy, as embodiment of the forces of modernity threatening to destroy the authentic national community.

The publication of the *Protocols of the Elders of Zion* in Russia in 1903 — a fabricated text purporting to be the minutes of a Jewish conspiracy for world domination — and its subsequent worldwide circulation, translation, and exploitation by political movements from czarist Russia to Nazi Germany to the Arab world, is one of the most consequential and most disgraceful episodes in the history of political propaganda. The text was exposed as a plagiarism and a forgery as early as 1921, yet it continued to circulate and to shape the imagination of antisemites around the world.

The Dreyfus Affair in France (1894–1906), in which a Jewish army officer was falsely convicted of treason amidst a storm of antisemitic propaganda, demonstrated that even in the birthplace of Jewish emancipation, the promise of civic equality remained fragile. It was the spectacle of the Dreyfus Affair that convinced Theodor Herzl, an assimilated Viennese Jewish journalist, that the solution to the "Jewish problem" could not be found within the framework of European nationalism and that the Jewish people needed a state of their own — a conviction that launched the Zionist movement and set in motion the chain of events that would lead, fifty years later, to the establishment of the State of Israel.

